    "difficulty": "EASY",
    "stem": "Find the principal value of \\(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\).",
    "solution": "[Textbook misprint: the printed solution gives the principal-value branch of sin inverse as the OPEN interval, but the endpoints are attained, so the branch is the CLOSED interval. The book contradicts itself here — its own section 2.2, its Summary table, and its answer key for Ex 2.1 Q13 all give the closed interval. The answer to this example is unaffected.] Let \\(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right) = y\\). Then, \\(\\sin y = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\).\n\nWe know that the range of the principal value branch of \\(\\sin^{-1}\\) is \\(\\left(\\frac{-\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\). Therefore, principal value of \\(\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)\\) is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).",
    "_note": "The book prints the range of the principal value branch of sin^-1 here with ROUND brackets, i.e. (-pi/2, pi/2). That contradicts the book's own Section 2.2 (printed p.19) and its Summary table (printed p.32), both of which give the CLOSED interval [-pi/2, pi/2] — which is correct, since sin^-1(1) = pi/2 and sin^-1(-1) = -pi/2 are attained. Transcribed as printed; the stated answer pi/4 is unaffected."
  },
